-- 047: Deep content + matching exercises for X11 · Storytelling with Data. Upscale.
UPDATE public.sessions SET
 content_en = E'> 🚀 **Upscale** session — available to Extended-track learners.\n\n## Analysis is worthless if it does not land\n\nThe best analysis dies in a cluttered slide nobody understands. Storytelling is how you move an audience from data to **decision**. This lesson is about structuring and delivering findings so they act.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Start with the audience\n\nWho is in the room decides what you show:\n\n| Audience | Wants | Give them |\n|----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Executive** | The decision & the ask | Conclusion first, 1–2 supporting charts |\n| **Manager** | What to do this week | Recommendations + owners |\n| **Analyst/peer** | The method | Methodology, assumptions, detail |\n| **Finance** | The money | ROI, cost, payback |\n\n> Same analysis, different deck. Never dump the analyst-level detail on an executive.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Pyramid Principle (Minto)\n\nLead with the **answer**, then support it:\n\n```\nConclusion → "Cut discounts on Electronics; it will lift margin 4pts"\n↓\nKey reasons → returns rise with discount; Electronics has thin margin\n↓\nEvidence → the charts and numbers behind each reason\n```\n\nBusy stakeholders get the point in the first sentence; the detail is there if they want to drill down.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Duarte narrative arc\n\nGreat talks move between **what is** and **what could be**:\n\n`What is (the problem today) → What could be (the better future) → Call to action`\n\nThe gap between "is" and "could be" creates the tension that makes people want to act.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slide design principles\n\n- **One message per slide** — write it as the slide title ("Returns doubled after the March promo"), not a topic label.\n- **The visual supports the sentence** — remove anything that does not.\n- **Do not repeat** a chart and its table; pick the one that makes the point.\n- **Highlight the point** — one colour on the bar that matters; grey the rest.\n\n> **Mini case study.** A 25-slide "data dump" got no decision. Rebuilt to 6 slides — one conclusion slide, three reason slides, one ROI slide, one ask — it got budget approval in ten minutes. Same data, story structure.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- **Burying the conclusion** on slide 20.\n- **Wall-of-text slides** the presenter just reads aloud.\n- **Chart junk** — 3D, gradients, redundant legends.\n- **No ask** — ending on a chart instead of "here is what we should do".\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| Pyramid Principle | Conclusion first, then supporting reasons & evidence |\n| Narrative arc | Movement between "what is" and "what could be" |\n| One message per slide | Each slide makes exactly one point |\n| Call to action | The explicit next step you are asking for |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. Tailor the deck to the **audience** — executives get the decision, peers get the method.\n2. Use the **Pyramid Principle**: conclusion first, then reasons, then evidence.\n3. Build tension with the **what-is → what-could-be** arc and end on a **call to action**.\n4. **One message per slide**, stated in the title; the visual supports it.\n5. Cut chart junk and never bury the conclusion.\n\n> 💡 Practice with the matching exercises below, then structure a stakeholder deck for the assignment.',
 content_id = E'> 🚀 Sesi **Upscale** — tersedia untuk peserta jalur Extended.\n\n## Analisis tak berguna kalau tidak tersampaikan\n\nAnalisis terbaik mati di slide berantakan yang tak dimengerti siapa pun. Storytelling adalah cara memindahkan audiens dari data ke **keputusan**. Pelajaran ini soal menyusun dan menyampaikan temuan agar mereka bertindak.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mulai dari audiens\n\nSiapa yang di ruangan menentukan apa yang Anda tampilkan:\n\n| Audiens | Ingin | Beri mereka |\n|---------|-------|-------------|\n| **Eksekutif** | Keputusan & permintaannya | Kesimpulan dulu, 1–2 chart pendukung |\n| **Manajer** | Apa yang dilakukan minggu ini | Rekomendasi + pemilik |\n| **Analyst/rekan** | Metodenya | Metodologi, asumsi, detail |\n| **Finance** | Uangnya | ROI, biaya, payback |\n\n> Analisis sama, deck berbeda. Jangan pernah menumpahkan detail level analyst ke eksekutif.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pyramid Principle (Minto)\n\nMulai dengan **jawaban**, lalu dukung:\n\n```\nKesimpulan → "Kurangi diskon Electronics; margin naik 4 poin"\n↓\nAlasan utama → retur naik seiring diskon; margin Electronics tipis\n↓\nBukti → chart dan angka di balik tiap alasan\n```\n\nStakeholder sibuk menangkap poin di kalimat pertama; detail tersedia bila ingin menyelami.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Narrative arc Duarte\n\nPresentasi hebat bergerak antara **yang ada** dan **yang bisa terjadi**:\n\n`Yang ada (masalah hari ini) → Yang bisa terjadi (masa depan lebih baik) → Call to action`\n\nJarak antara "yang ada" dan "yang bisa terjadi" menciptakan ketegangan yang membuat orang ingin bertindak.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Prinsip desain slide\n\n- **Satu pesan per slide** — tulis sebagai judul slide ("Retur berlipat setelah promo Maret"), bukan label topik.\n- **Visual mendukung kalimat** — buang apa pun yang tidak.\n- **Jangan mengulang** chart dan tabelnya; pilih yang menyampaikan poin.\n- **Sorot poinnya** — satu warna pada bar yang penting; abu-abukan sisanya.\n\n> **Studi kasus mini.** "Data dump" 25 slide tak menghasilkan keputusan. Dibangun ulang jadi 6 slide — satu slide kesimpulan, tiga slide alasan, satu slide ROI, satu permintaan — anggaran disetujui dalam sepuluh menit. Data sama, struktur cerita.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum\n\n- **Mengubur kesimpulan** di slide 20.\n- **Slide penuh teks** yang tinggal dibacakan presenter.\n- **Chart junk** — 3D, gradien, legenda berlebihan.\n- **Tanpa permintaan** — berakhir di chart alih-alih "inilah yang harus kita lakukan".\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| Pyramid Principle | Kesimpulan dulu, lalu alasan & bukti pendukung |\n| Narrative arc | Gerak antara "yang ada" dan "yang bisa terjadi" |\n| Satu pesan per slide | Tiap slide menyampaikan tepat satu poin |\n| Call to action | Langkah berikutnya yang eksplisit Anda minta |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. Sesuaikan deck dengan **audiens** — eksekutif dapat keputusan, rekan dapat metode.\n2. Pakai **Pyramid Principle**: kesimpulan dulu, lalu alasan, lalu bukti.\n3. Bangun ketegangan dengan arc **yang-ada → yang-bisa-terjadi** dan akhiri dengan **call to action**.\n4. **Satu pesan per slide**, dinyatakan di judul; visual mendukungnya.\n5. Buang chart junk dan jangan pernah mengubur kesimpulan.\n\n> 💡 Latih dengan exercise matching di bawah, lalu susun deck stakeholder untuk tugas.'
WHERE session_number = 'X11';

INSERT INTO public.exercises (session_id, type, title_en, title_id, description_en, description_id, starter_code, solution_code, test_cases, hints_en, hints_id, difficulty, order_num)
SELECT s.id, 'matching', 'Storytelling Terms', 'Istilah Storytelling',
  'Match each term to its meaning.', 'Cocokkan setiap istilah dengan artinya.',
  '{"pairs":[{"left":"Pyramid Principle","right":"Conclusion first, then support"},{"left":"Narrative arc","right":"Move between what is and what could be"},{"left":"One message per slide","right":"Each slide makes one point"},{"left":"Call to action","right":"The explicit next step you ask for"}]}','',
  '[{"id":"tc1","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Conclusion first, then support","description_en":"Pyramid","description_id":"Pyramid","points":25},{"id":"tc2","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Move between what is and what could be","description_en":"Arc","description_id":"Arc","points":25},{"id":"tc3","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Each slide makes one point","description_en":"One message","description_id":"Satu pesan","points":25},{"id":"tc4","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"The explicit next step you ask for","description_en":"CTA","description_id":"CTA","points":25}]',
  '["Pyramid leads with the answer","A CTA asks for a decision"]','["Pyramid mulai dari jawaban","CTA meminta keputusan"]','easy',1
FROM public.sessions s WHERE s.session_number='X11' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.exercises e WHERE e.session_id=s.id AND e.title_en='Storytelling Terms');

